\begin_layout Title
Securing Open Sound Control (OSC) over UDP:
 A Lightweight HMAC-Based Authentication Scheme for Embedded Systems
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Paul V.
 Miller
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This project presents a lightweight authentication layer for transmitting OSC (Open Sound Control) packets over UDP,
 using HMAC and sequence-based replay protection.
 The approach is designed for resource-constrained embedded controllers that operate in live performance or real-time musical applications.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Background
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
OSC
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
OSC (Open Sound Control) is widely used in computer music and interactive computer systems for sending live performance data efficiently.
 Very frequently the data is some musical control information (such as pitch,
 glide or velocity) that is produced by some sort of human interface.
 Many OSC software controllers are available online such as TouchOSC or Protokol,
 that will work on a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

smart
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

-phone.
 These software implementations typically allow the user to design an interface that can include virtual buttons,
 sliders,
 knobs,
 or other kinds of control surfaces that send data to a synthesizer or other receiving application.
 A typical setup is shown below.
\end_layout

