\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
that stipulates 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

the first datum is an integer,
 the second is a float and the third is a string
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 The type tag always begins with a comma.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
There are several OSC type tags in the protocol specs.
 To summarize:
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

numeric:
 i,
 f,
 h,
 d (integer,
 float,
 8-byte int,
 8-byte float
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

text/binary:
 s,
 b:
 string,
 blob (a binary blob can be of arbitrary length,
 and can transmit audio chunks,
 etc.)
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

special:
 T,
 F,
 N,
 I:
 these do not have any corresponding data bytes – the type tag alone carries information (True,
 False,
 Null,
 Infinity)
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

structure:
 t,
 m,
 r (timetag,
 MIDI,
 color)
\end_layout

\end_inset

 So in the case above,
 we must do the following:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
first send the address string 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\family typewriter
/controller/move
\family default

\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

,
 which is null-terminated and padded to a multiple of 4 bytes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Next we send the type tag string 
\family typewriter

\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

,ifs
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\family default
,
 which must always begin with a comma.
 It is also null-terminated and padded to a multiple of 4 bytes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Finally we can send the arguments.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Integers are 32-bit signed,
 and need to be big-endian (network byte order);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Floats are 32-bit IEEE 754 standard floating-point,
 and also big-endian;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Strings are standard ASCII and must be null-terminated,
 and padded to 4 bytes,
 so 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

hello
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 in OSC is 8 bytes.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
These standards decrease the potential ambiguity in sending OSC messages.
 The idea behind OSC was to improve upon the MIDI standard which,
 though useful in many cases,
 is limited to 7-bit values 0-127.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The most significant bit in MIDI is used to distinguish between status bytes and data bytes,
 allowing the system to distinguish between 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

starting a new message
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 (status byte) or 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

this byte is a continuation of the current message
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 (data).
 The designers of MIDI understood that there would probably only be 127 discrete values for pitch and volume,
 so they left it at that;
 the MIDI standard becomes more problematic when users require sending other kinds of performance data.
\end_layout

\end_inset

OSC also works well with patching environments such as Max/MSP and Pd,
 because message tree structures can be very easily separated using the 
\family typewriter
[route]
\family default
 object.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
While OSC has been widely adopted,
 it is not without its critics.
 See 
