\begin_layout Subsection
Materials
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this project I used four Raspberry Pi Pico W boards and standard,
 low-cost electronics components such as breadboards,
 jumper cables,
 buttons,
 potentiometers,
 and simple LEDs.
 A separate WiFi router was necessary,
 since DuqNet does not allow ad-hoc UDP.
 I built two victim Pico setups and two attackers to demonstrate unsecured and secured models.
 The code was written in Python.
 The systems are shown below:
